    "summary": "Stellar Liberty & Crazy Fingers; tight Bertha→Greatest opener",
    "blurb": "The show shines with a soaring Liberty and an unforgettable Crazy Fingers solo. Jerry's acoustic‑MIDI tone dazzles, and the first set opens with a crisp Bertha → Greatest transition. The second set maintains high energy throughout.",
    "review": "Feb 21, 1993 at the Oakland Coliseum captures the Dead fresh off a month’s break, firing on all cylinders. Jerry Garcia is in top form – his acoustic‑MIDI work on “Liberty” glitters and his solo on “Crazy Fingers” has been called iconic by multiple listeners. The band kicks things off with a rare, tightly executed Bertha → Greatest Story Ever Told segue that sets an upbeat tone for the night. Throughout the second set the energy stays high, highlighted by “Eternity” and a solid run through the classics, while even the stumble on “Lazy River Road” feels like a minor blip in an otherwise vibrant performance.\n\nAudience recordings dominate the available sources, with the Seff tape (gd93-02-21.aud.seff.1055.sbeok.shnf) praised for its clean balance and crisp crowd ambience – enough that fans wish there were a soundboard version to match. While one SBD entry offers no concrete commentary, the consensus across the audience tapes is clear: this night delivers strong musicianship, memorable moments, and an overall cohesive flow that makes it a worthwhile listen for any Deadhead.",
